c RUN: %solver --anfread %s --anfwrite /dev/stdout --xl 0 --el 0 --sat 0 --binomred 0 --shorten 0 --probe 0 --monogauss 0 --gb 0 | %OutputCheck %s
c RUN: %solver --anfread %s --solve --allsol --verb 0 | %verify allsol %s
c
c 1 + p is a product of linear factors, so p = 0 says every factor is 1:
c 1 + (x1*x2 + x1 + 1) = x1*(x2 + 1): x1 = 1 and x2 = 0.
c 1 + (x3*x4 + x3*x5 + x4*x6 + x5*x6 + 1) = (x3 + x6)*(x4 + x5): two XORs,
c x3 + x6 = 1 and x4 + x5 = 1, which are anti-equivalences.
c x7 + x8 + x9 + 1 = 0 is linear and left alone.
x1*x2 + x1 + 1
x3*x4 + x3*x5 + x4*x6 + x5*x6 + 1
x7 + x8 + x9 + 1
c CHECK: \[prod-split\] split 2 eqs into 4 linear ones
c CHECK-L: Fixed values
c CHECK-L: x(1) + 1
c CHECK-L: x(2)
c CHECK-L: Equivalences
c CHECK-L: x(6) + x(3) + 1
c CHECK-L: x(5) + x(4) + 1
